<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What goes into the account's backup when BACKUP TO GOOGLE is on, and what
  never does.

  The rule is that a restored install is the same machine, not a new one that
  happens to have some old files in it. Delete the app, reinstall it with the
  backup on, and it comes back as it was: the boxes, the histories, every
  setting, and the link to the game's sync account, so it goes on syncing with
  the game without anybody re-entering a code. The app asks once, before
  anything else is on screen, whether that is what was wanted — see
  RestoreScreen.

  Shared preferences carry the settings and the sync credentials and are all
  included, which is the point above.

  What is left out is everything that is either re-fetchable or about this
  device alone. That is not tidiness: the platform allows twenty-five
  megabytes per app and drops the *whole* backup, silently, when an app is
  over it. Anything big enough to matter has to be named here.

    * roms/ is the player's own cartridge dumps — one to two megabytes each,
      six of them, which is most of the allowance on its own. They are
      imported from files the player already has, they are the one thing in
      here that is not this app's to copy anywhere, and IMPORT ROM puts them
      back in a tap.
    * sprites/, cries/, followers/ and trainers/ are downloaded art, hundreds
      of megabytes of it, and they come back with a tap.
    * pc/backups holds the copy taken of a cartridge before each write, which
      is evidence about this device and belongs to it.
    * install.marker is how a restore is noticed at all: it is written on
      first run and never allowed into a backup, so boxes arriving without it
      are boxes that came from somewhere else. backup.marker and
      restore.marker are the same kind of thing — when this device last had a
      backup taken and when it last had one put back — and describe this
      device rather than the data.
    * last-crash.txt is about a crash on the old phone.

  pc/lineages.lua does go, and has to: it is where a Pokémon that is out in a
  cartridge keeps its history, and a history that did not survive a new phone
  would not be a history.

  With the switch off, StorageBackupAgent writes nothing at all and none of
  this applies.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <exclude domain="file" path="roms/" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="sprites/" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="cries/" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="followers/" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="trainers/" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="pc/backups/" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="install.marker" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="backup.marker" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="restore.marker" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="drive-backup.marker" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="last-crash.txt" />
</full-backup-content>
